1
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:09,565
Deep dive: nesting. How parts are laid out on sheets, which settings drive it and why, and how to fix a layout by hand.

2
00:00:09,565 --> 00:00:20,075
The Novak kitchen nests onto six 5 x 12 ft sheets. One material per sheet, so the strip shows the board and how full each sheet is.

3
00:00:20,075 --> 00:00:30,989
Sheet 4, white melamine, 93% used. Under the name: the trim at the sheet edge and the spacing between parts.

4
00:00:30,989 --> 00:00:44,973
Those come from Machine & tools, Nesting. Part spacing is the cut-out tool (1/2") plus a little extra, so the bit has room between parts.

5
00:00:44,973 --> 00:00:53,843
Edge trim: factory sheet edges are often chipped or a hair out of square, so the nest stays 3/8" in from every edge.

6
00:00:53,843 --> 00:01:03,861
Allow 90° rotation lets parts turn to fit better. On a grained board, grain-locked parts never turn, so oak doors keep their grain vertical.

7
00:01:03,861 --> 00:01:12,774
Nesting engine: Best of both runs the rectangle and true-shape nesters and keeps whichever uses fewer sheets.

8
00:01:12,774 --> 00:01:22,239
Keep kits on one sheet keeps a cabinet’s parts together, handy for assembling box by box, at the cost of a little yield.

9
00:01:22,239 --> 00:01:32,882
Offcuts: pieces at least this long and wide are kept as remnants, not scrap. Use stock offcuts first fills saved remnants before new sheets.

10
00:01:32,882 --> 00:01:47,742
Grain lives on the material. In the Library, the oak board has grain along the sheet length; white melamine has none, so its parts may turn.

11
00:01:47,742 --> 00:02:11,611
Back to the job. The Area and cost table splits each sheet: parts, remnants kept and scrap. That is your real yield.

12
00:02:11,611 --> 00:02:21,054
The job leaves offcuts. After cutting, Update offcut stock adds them to the library, so the next job can use them first.

13
00:02:21,054 --> 00:02:32,848
Edit layout to move parts by hand, e.g. to keep the two halves of a pair on one sheet, or to free a sheet for a remnant.

14
00:02:32,848 --> 00:02:46,746
Drag a part: it snaps to the nest spacing, the trim and its neighbours. The live check flags any overlap, spacing, trim or grain problem at once.

15
00:02:46,746 --> 00:03:16,776
Turn, End for end and Move to sheet… work on the picked part. Undo steps back; here we Cancel and keep the automatic nest.

16
00:03:16,776 --> 00:03:26,682
Why it matters: a saved layout drives the programs, labels and export check. A sheet you never touched says “nested automatically” and re-nests when the job changes; a saved one stays put.

17
00:03:26,682 --> 00:03:31,965
So: set spacing, trim and rotation once for your machine, let grain protect the show faces, and only hand-edit when you have a reason.
